@*
    The facet panel: one plain GET form, no script.

    The querystring *is* the state, so a filtered listing is linkable, the back button works, and the
    server recomputes every count on every request. Nothing here is a control that remembers
    something — there is no state anywhere but the URL, which is what makes a shared link the same
    page for the person who receives it.

    Every count beside a value came from the same pass that produced the listing below it (see
    FacetedSearch), so a choice cannot promise results it will not deliver, and a value nothing
    matches is never drawn at all.

    It used to explain itself in a paragraph under the controls, and every facet wore a sentence for
    a label — "codebase the game says so". The facts in that prose are all still here; none of them
    is prose any more. The measured/declared split is a two-word chip on each group, the counts speak
    for themselves, the one reading that has to be prevented at the moment of ticking sits inside the
    fieldset it applies to, and the rest is one disclosure a reader can open once and never again.
*@

<form method="get" action="/games" role="search" class="facet-form">
    @*
        The bar: the whole question in one row — what to match, what order to read it in, and whether
        the archive is in. Everything here is a control a reader touches on most visits; the facets
        below are the ones they touch on some.
    *@
    <div class="filter-bar">
        <label for="q" class="sr-only">Search games</label>
        <input id="q" type="search" name="@FacetKeys.Text" value="@Filter.Text"
               placeholder="search by name, description or codebase" />

        <span class="bar-field">
            <label for="sort" class="bar-label">sort</label>
            @*
                Grouped by what each order reads — a fact already on the row, or a statistic over a
                span — because nine options in one flat list is a list nobody reads to the bottom of,
                and because that split is the difference a reader most needs to see before choosing.
                Enumerated from the enum rather than listed here, so a sort added there cannot be one
                the panel silently stops offering.
            *@
            <select id="sort" name="@FacetKeys.Sort">
                @foreach (var group in Enum.GetValues<GameSort>().GroupBy(FacetWords.SortGroup))
                {
                    <optgroup label="@group.Key">
                        @foreach (var sort in group)
                        {
                            <option value="@FacetTokens.Of(sort)" selected="@(Filter.Sort == sort)">
                                @FacetWords.Sort(sort)
                            </option>
                        }
                    </optgroup>
                }
            </select>
        </span>

        <label class="check bar-check">
            <input type="checkbox" name="@FacetKeys.Archived" value="true" checked="@Filter.IncludeArchived" />
            <span>include archived</span>
        </label>

        @*
            The one exclusion this listing applies that the reader did not ask for, and the control
            that undoes it, in the bar on every request rather than behind a preference. It is a
            default of ours over what a game declared about itself — never a measurement of one —
            so the way out of it has to be as visible as the listing it shapes.
        *@
        <label class="check bar-check">
            <input type="checkbox" name="@FacetKeys.Adult" value="true" checked="@Filter.IncludeAdult" />
            <span>include adult</span>
        </label>

        <button type="submit">show</button>
    </div>

    @if (Facets.Count > 0)
    {
        @*
            One row per value, three states: off · only · anything but.

            It was one <select> per facet holding every value twice — once under "only", once under
            "anything but" — which is thirteen values rendered as twenty-seven options, read out as
            near-identical pairs, and a state nothing showed at rest: a select sitting on an option
            in its second group looks exactly like one sitting on "any" until it is opened.

            Two links per row instead — tick to include, − to exclude — and each one is this listing
            asked one facet differently. They apply on click, which no scriptless control on a form
            can do: as radios they were honest about the filter's shape and useless in the hand,
            because nothing happened until the reader found the "show" button.

            The querystring is unchanged and is still the whole of the state: the exclusion is the
            same !value token the binding and the read API already parse, and a ticked row's own
            link is the one that clears it.
        *@
        <div class="facet-grid">
            @foreach (var group in Facets.Where(g => g.Kind is FacetKind.Choice))
            {
                <fieldset class="facet choice @(group.IsFiltered ? "chosen" : null)">
                    <legend>
                        <span class="facet-name">@FacetWords.Group(group.Key)</span>
                        @Evidence(group)
                    </legend>

                    <div class="rows">
                        @*
                            Always first, always empty: a facet you cannot un-choose is a trap. It
                            is a row like the others rather than a "clear" button, because "any" is
                            an answer to the same question the rows below it answer.
                        *@
                        <div class="facet-row any @(group.IsFiltered ? null : "on")">
                            <a class="pick" href="@Href(group, null)"
                               aria-current="@(group.IsFiltered ? null : "true")"
                               aria-label="@($"any {FacetWords.Group(group.Key)}, {group.Total} games")">
                                <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true">@(group.IsFiltered ? "☐" : "✓")</span>
                                <span class="row-name">any</span>
                                <span class="count">@group.Total</span>
                            </a>
                        </div>

                        @foreach (var value in Shown(group))
                        {
                            @Row(group, value)
                        }
                    </div>

                    @if (Rest(group) is { Count: > 0 } rest)
                    {
                        @*
                            The tail, behind a native disclosure — open when the reader's own choice
                            is in it, because a selection nothing shows is the defect this replaces.
                            Closed or open, the controls are in the form and are submitted either
                            way.
                        *@
                        <details class="facet-tail" open="@rest.Any(v => v.IsSelected)">
                            <summary>@rest.Count more</summary>
                            <div class="rows">
                                @foreach (var value in rest)
                                {
                                    @Row(group, value)
                                }
                            </div>
                        </details>
                    }
                </fieldset>
            }
        </div>

        @* Said once, under the groups it applies to, rather than on each of them. *@
        <p class="hint facet-hint">Tick to include, − to exclude.</p>

        @foreach (var group in Facets.Where(g => g.Kind is FacetKind.Presence))
        {
            <fieldset class="facet presence">
                <legend>
                    <span class="facet-name">@FacetWords.Group(group.Key)</span>
                    @Evidence(group)
                </legend>
                <div class="checks">
                    @foreach (var value in group.Values)
                    {
                        <label class="check">
                            <input type="checkbox" name="@group.Key" value="@value.Token"
                                   checked="@value.IsSelected" />
                            <span class="check-name">@FacetWords.Value(group.Key, value)</span>
                            <span class="count">@value.Count</span>
                        </label>
                    }
                </div>
                @*
                    The one sentence that has to be readable at the moment of ticking rather than in
                    a key somewhere else, because a reader ticking boxes is exactly the person who
                    would otherwise read an unticked box as the game declining a protocol.
                *@
                <p class="hint">Unticked means not measured — not that the game lacks it.</p>
            </fieldset>
        }
    }

    @*
        The key. Two words and their meanings, said once, where the panel used to say them seven
        times; and one disclosure holding the readings that would otherwise have to be guessed. Not a
        tooltip and not a title attribute: this is the difference the site exists to publish, so it
        stays in the document whether or not a pointer ever hovers anything.
    *@
    <div class="facet-key">
        <span class="key-item">
            <span class="evidence measured"><span aria-hidden="true">●</span> @FacetWords.Evidence(FacetEvidence.Measured)</span>
            @FacetWords.EvidenceMeaning(FacetEvidence.Measured)
        </span>
        <span class="key-item">
            <span class="evidence declared"><span aria-hidden="true">◇</span> @FacetWords.Evidence(FacetEvidence.Declared)</span>
            @FacetWords.EvidenceMeaning(FacetEvidence.Declared)
        </span>
        <span class="key-item">
            <span class="evidence derived"><span aria-hidden="true">◆</span> @FacetWords.Evidence(FacetEvidence.Derived)</span>
            @FacetWords.EvidenceMeaning(FacetEvidence.Derived)
        </span>
        <span class="key-item">counts are exact, from the same query as the list below</span>

        <details class="facet-more">
            <summary>what a blank means</summary>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    A blank is a gap in our measurement, not a no. Each facet spells its own:
                    <em>not identified</em>, <em>not declared</em>, <em>nothing negotiated</em>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A measured zero is a count. An unknown count is not a zero and never sorts as one.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Open-ended facets list their @FacetedSearch.MaxValues commonest values. The rest
                    are reachable by search and by URL.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </details>
    </div>
</form>

@*
    What the query is asking for, repeated where the results are, one removable chip each.

    Outside the form on purpose: these are links rather than controls, and each undoes exactly one
    selection while leaving every other in place — which no <select> on the panel can do. It is also
    the only place the third state is legible at rest, since a <select> scrolled to an option in its
    second <optgroup> shows nothing until it is opened.
*@
@if (Chips.Count > 0)
{
    <div class="active-filters">
        <span class="kicker">showing</span>
        @foreach (var chip in Chips)
        {
            <a class="filter-chip" href="@chip.RemoveHref">
                <span class="chip-facet">@chip.Facet</span>
                <span class="chip-value">@chip.Value</span>
                <span class="chip-x" aria-hidden="true">✕</span>
                <span class="sr-only">— stop filtering by this</span>
            </a>
        }
        <a class="clear-all" href="/games">clear all</a>
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// How many values a facet shows before the rest go behind a disclosure.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A facet may hold twelve values (<see cref="FacetedSearch.MaxValues"/>) and there are nine
    /// facets: rendered in full, the panel would be taller than the listing it filters. Five is
    /// enough to show what a facet is about — they arrive commonest-first — and the tail is one
    /// keystroke away rather than a scroll.
    /// </remarks>
    private const int RowsShown = 5;

    /// <summary>The values with a row of their own, and the ones behind the disclosure.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A selected value is always in the visible half, whatever its count: a reader's own choice
    /// scrolled out of sight is the defect this whole control replaces.
    /// </remarks>
    private static IReadOnlyList<FacetValue> Shown(FacetGroup group)
    {
        var head = group.Values.Take(RowsShown).ToList();

        // Appended rather than promoted, so choosing a value out of the tail does not reorder the
        // rows under the reader's pointer — the list they just used is the list they get back.
        head.AddRange(group.Values.Skip(RowsShown).Where(v => v.IsSelected));

        return head;
    }

    private static IReadOnlyList<FacetValue> Rest(FacetGroup group) =>
        [.. group.Values.Except(Shown(group))];

    /// <summary>
    /// One value, as the two states it can be asked for and the one it is in.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// Both radios carry an <c>aria-label</c> holding all three parts of the fact — the value, its
    /// count and what ticking it would do — because the visible row says them in three separate
    /// elements and a screen reader arrowing through a radio group hears the control, not the row.
    /// The visible name is inside the accessible one, so a reader saying "tick PennMUSH" to a voice
    /// control is understood.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The exclusion's count is what excluding actually returns — everything but this value — and
    /// not the value's own, which would offer a number the listing will not produce.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private RenderFragment Row(FacetGroup group, FacetValue value) =>
        @<div class="facet-row @RowState(value)">
            <a class="pick" href="@Href(group, value.State is FacetState.Included ? null : value.Token)"
               aria-current="@(value.State is FacetState.Included ? "true" : null)"
               aria-label="@($"{FacetWords.Value(group.Key, value)}, {value.Count} games, only")">
                <span class="tick" aria-hidden="true">@(value.State is FacetState.Included ? "✓" : "☐")</span>
                <span class="row-name">@FacetWords.Value(group.Key, value)</span>
                <span class="count">@value.Count</span>
            </a>
            <a class="drop"
               href="@Href(group, value.State is FacetState.Excluded ? null : Excluded(value).Token)"
               aria-current="@(value.State is FacetState.Excluded ? "true" : null)"
               aria-label="@($"{FacetWords.Value(group.Key, value)}, {group.Total - value.Count} games, excluded")">
                <span aria-hidden="true">@(value.State is FacetState.Excluded ? "×" : "−")</span>
            </a>
        </div>;

    /// <summary>
    /// This listing, asked one facet differently — and asked nothing at all where the token is null,
    /// which is what makes a ticked row untick itself.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>They are links, not controls, and that is why they work on click.</b> As radios they were
    /// honest about the filter's shape and useless in the hand: a reader ticked a value and nothing
    /// happened until they found the "show" button, which is the failure a filter panel cannot have.
    /// A link needs no script, applies immediately, is a real target for the keyboard and the
    /// browser's own "open in a new tab", and the state it produces is the URL — which is what this
    /// panel has always said the state is.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The whole querystring rides along, so a facet change keeps the sort, the archive switch and
    /// plain mode. What it cannot keep is text typed into the search box and not yet submitted; that
    /// box has its own button, and no scriptless page can read an unsubmitted field.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private string Href(FacetGroup group, string? token) =>
        "/games" + ListingLinks.With(Query, group.Key, token);

    private static string RowState(FacetValue value) => value.State switch
    {
        FacetState.Included => "on",
        FacetState.Excluded => "excluded",
        _ => "off",
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// The same value, spelled as an exclusion — <c>Evennia</c> becomes <c>!Evennia</c>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Built through <see cref="FacetChoice"/> rather than by prefixing a string here, so the
    /// panel's spelling and the binding's parser cannot drift: a value that itself begins with the
    /// marker is escaped by the same code that unescapes it.
    /// </remarks>
    private static FacetChoice Excluded(FacetValue value) =>
        (value.IsUnknown ? FacetChoice.Unknown : FacetChoice.Of(value.Token)) with { Exclude = true };

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<FacetGroup> Facets { get; set; } = [];

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public GameFilter Filter { get; set; } = new();

    /// <summary>
    /// The querystring this listing is answering, which is what a chip needs to remove itself from.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Optional, and an empty one only costs the chips: with no query there is nothing selected, so
    /// there is nothing to draw. The panel itself needs no URL — it is a form, and the browser builds
    /// the next one out of the controls.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public string Query { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    private IReadOnlyList<ActiveFilter> Chips => ActiveFilters.For(Facets, Filter, Query);

    /// <summary>
    /// The evidence chip: a glyph, and the word, always both.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The glyphs are the site's own state vocabulary — ● for something we watched, ◇ for something
    /// only claimed, ◆ for a claim we grouped ourselves — and they are <c>aria-hidden</c>, because
    /// the word beside them is the same fact and a screen reader announcing "black circle measured"
    /// is worse than announcing "measured". Colour is never the carrier: the word is legible with no
    /// ink at all, which is also why the third glyph is the second one filled in rather than a shape
    /// nobody has seen before — derived is a declaration with our hand on it, and reads as one.
    /// </remarks>
    private static RenderFragment Evidence(FacetGroup group) => @<span class="evidence @Kind(group)">
        <span aria-hidden="true">@Glyph(group.Evidence)</span>
        @FacetWords.Evidence(group.Evidence)
    </span>;

    private static string Glyph(FacetEvidence evidence) => evidence switch
    {
        FacetEvidence.Measured => "●",
        FacetEvidence.Derived => "◆",
        _ => "◇",
    };

    private static string Kind(FacetGroup group) => group.Evidence switch
    {
        FacetEvidence.Measured => "measured",
        FacetEvidence.Derived => "derived",
        _ => "declared",
    };
}
